   $q$A classmate says sentence 4 does not prove students ate more, because the school might have simply cooked less food. Which detail from the article would best answer this concern?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Delgado notes that students voted on the recipes during their lunch period.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"does-not-address-concern"},{"text":"Delgado notes that the new recipes included a bean soup and a rice bowl.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"irrelevant-evidence"},{"text":"Delgado notes that each meal still had to cost less than three dollars.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"supports-different-point"},{"text":"Delgado notes that the school cooked the same number of meals before and after the change.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Delgado notes that the school cooked the same number of meals before and after the change.$q$, $q$If the same number of meals was cooked and less was thrown away, then students really did eat more, which answers the classmate. Knowing when students voted does not show whether the amount of food cooked changed.$q$, 'draft', '1f43b476-1777-55fc-a3eb-dd2219c2ef77')
