   $q$In the sentence "The coach praised the team's quick, accurate passing," why is there a comma between "quick" and "accurate"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Every pair of adjectives before a noun must be separated by a comma.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralizes-comma-rule"},{"text":"A comma always comes before the final word that describes a noun.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"invented-final-adjective-rule"},{"text":"The comma shows that \"quick\" is describing the word \"accurate.\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"adjective-modifies-adjective"},{"text":"Each adjective describes \"passing\" equally, so they could be joined by \"and.\"","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Each adjective describes "passing" equally, so they could be joined by "and."$q$, $q$"Quick" and "accurate" are coordinate adjectives: "quick and accurate passing" makes sense, and so does "accurate, quick passing." Not every pair needs a comma, since cumulative adjectives like "small stone bridge" take none.$q$, 'draft', null)
