  ('280da5f7-6245-5c79-a3ad-c16f4b4cd45b', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g7-capitalization-punctuation'), null, 'hard',
   $q$In the sentence "Lena packed a heavy winter coat for the trip," why is there no comma between "heavy" and "winter"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Heavy\" is not an adjective in this sentence, so it needs no comma.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misidentifies-part-of-speech"},{"text":"\"Winter coat\" works as a unit, and \"heavy and winter coat\" does not make sense.","is_correct":true},{"text":"A comma is needed only when three or more adjectives come before a noun.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"invented-three-adjective-rule"},{"text":"Commas are never used between two adjectives before a noun.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralizes-no-comma"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Winter coat" works as a unit, and "heavy and winter coat" does not make sense.$q$, $q$"Heavy" describes the whole phrase "winter coat," so the adjectives are cumulative and cannot take "and" or be switched. Saying commas are never used between adjectives is wrong, since coordinate adjectives like "warm, cozy" do take one.$q$, 'draft', null)
