        '6-8', $q$The Pony Express$q$, $q$(1) In 1860, a letter sent from Missouri to California could take weeks by stagecoach or months by ship. (2) A company decided to try a faster method called the Pony Express. (3) It was a mail service in which riders on horseback carried letters across about 1,900 miles of plains, mountains, and desert.

(4) The system worked like a relay race. (5) Stations were built roughly every ten to fifteen miles along the route. (6) At each station, a rider jumped onto a fresh horse and kept going. (7) After about seventy-five miles, he handed the mail pouch to a new rider. (8) Riders traveled day and night in all kinds of weather. (9) As a result, a letter could cross the whole route in about ten days.

(10) The service was expensive, though. (11) At first, sending a half-ounce letter cost five dollars, which was a large amount of money at the time. (12) Then, in October 1861, workers finished the first telegraph line across the country. (13) Messages could now travel in minutes over wires. (14) Two days later, the Pony Express announced that it would close. (15) Horses are still used today on many ranches.$q$, 'draft')
