        '6-8', $q$The School Safety Patrol$q$, $q$(1) Every morning at Lakeview School, a group of seventh and eighth graders in bright orange belts helps younger children get to class safely. (2) These students are members of the school safety patrol. (3) A safety patrol is a team of student volunteers who guide classmates at drop-off areas, bus loops, and hallways. (4) Patrol members do not direct cars, because that job belongs to adult crossing guards.

(5) Students must apply to join the patrol. (6) Teachers look for applicants who are responsible, on time, and calm when things get busy. (7) New members then attend a training session. (8) They learn hand signals, practice what to say to younger students, and study a map of the school grounds. (9) The cafeteria serves tacos on Fridays, which is many students' favorite lunch.

(10) Being on patrol teaches skills that last beyond middle school. (11) Members practice leadership when they remind a group of third graders to walk instead of run. (12) They build responsibility by arriving early every day, even in cold weather. (13) Many former patrol members say the job gave them the confidence to speak up in class.$q$, 'draft')
