-- Passage wg7-informative-writing-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2594b992-b8aa-5968-a43c-5d7b263e0eff', 'wg7-informative-writing-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g7-informative-writing'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Glow of Fireflies$q$, $q$(1) On summer evenings, small lights blink on and off above lawns and fields. (2) These lights come from fireflies, which are not actually flies at all. (3) They are a kind of beetle. (4) A firefly makes light through bioluminescence, which is the ability of a living thing to produce its own light. (5) Inside the firefly's lower body, chemicals mix with oxygen, and the reaction gives off a glow.

(6) Fireflies flash mainly to communicate. (7) In many species, males fly through the air and flash a pattern. (8) Females waiting in the grass answer with their own flashes. (9) Each species has its own pattern, so a firefly can recognize members of its own kind. (10) Some flash every few seconds, while others give quick double or triple blinks.

(11) The light a firefly makes is also very efficient. (12) An old-fashioned light bulb wastes much of its energy as heat. (13) Almost all of the energy in a firefly's reaction becomes light, so the insect's body stays cool. (14) For this reason, scientists sometimes call it "cold light."

(15) In some areas, people have noticed fewer fireflies than in the past. (16) Bright outdoor lights at night can make it harder for fireflies to see each other's signals.$q$, 'draft')
