   $q$Read the original sentence: "The committee voted to repair the playground, not to replace it, because the budget was small." Which shortened quotation uses an ellipsis without changing the writer's meaning?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"The committee voted to repair the playground ... because the budget was small.\"","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"The committee voted ... to replace it, because the budget was small.\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"omission-changes-meaning"},{"text":"\"The committee ... because the budget ... small.\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"omission-removes-key-idea"},{"text":"\"The committee voted to repair the playground because the budget was small.\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"omission-not-marked"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"The committee voted to repair the playground ... because the budget was small."$q$, $q$Leaving out "not to replace it" keeps the main point that the committee chose to repair the playground. The tempting choice that keeps "to replace it" flips the meaning, which an ellipsis must never do.$q$, 'draft', null)
