   $q$Only the students who turned in permission slips went on the field trip. Which sentence is punctuated to show that meaning?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The students, who had turned in permission slips, went on the field trip.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"nonessential-changes-meaning"},{"text":"The students who had turned in permission slips went on the field trip.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The students who had turned in permission slips, went on the field trip.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-between-subject-verb"},{"text":"The students, who had turned in permission slips went on the field trip.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"missing-closing-comma"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The students who had turned in permission slips went on the field trip.$q$, $q$Without commas, the clause tells which students went, so only those with slips went on the trip. With commas around the clause, it becomes extra information, and the sentence says that all the students went.$q$, 'draft', null)
