   $q$Read the original sentence: "The new skate park is popular with teenagers, but many neighbors say it is too noisy after dark." Which shortened quotation uses an ellipsis fairly, without changing the meaning?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"The new skate park is popular ... but many neighbors say it is too noisy after dark.\"","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"The new skate park is popular with teenagers, but neighbors say it is too noisy.\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"omission-not-marked"},{"text":"\"The new skate park ... is popular with teenagers, but many neighbors say it is too noisy after dark.\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ellipsis-nothing-omitted"},{"text":"\"The new skate park is popular with ... many neighbors.\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"omission-changes-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"The new skate park is popular ... but many neighbors say it is too noisy after dark."$q$, $q$Leaving out "with teenagers" keeps both ideas, that the park is popular and that neighbors find it noisy. The tempting short version makes it sound as if the neighbors like the park, which twists the original meaning.$q$, 'draft', null)
