   $q$Ms. Reyes and Mr. Park are the writer's coaches. Which sentence makes that meaning clear?$q$, $q$[{"text":"I want to thank my coaches Ms. Reyes, and Mr. Park for their help.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splits-names"},{"text":"I want to thank my coaches, Ms. Reyes, and Mr. Park for their help.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"serial-comma-makes-list"},{"text":"I want to thank Ms. Reyes and Mr. Park, my coaches, for their help.","is_correct":true},{"text":"I want to thank, my coaches Ms. Reyes and Mr. Park for their help.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-between-verb-object"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$I want to thank Ms. Reyes and Mr. Park, my coaches, for their help.$q$, $q$Setting off "my coaches" with commas right after the two names shows that the phrase renames Ms. Reyes and Mr. Park. The comma after "Ms. Reyes" in "my coaches, Ms. Reyes, and Mr. Park" turns the sentence into a list of three, as if the coaches were other people.$q$, 'draft', null)
