   $q$Which sentence is punctuated correctly?$q$, $q$[{"text":"In the spring of 2024, our town, which had never hosted a marathon welcomed nearly two thousand runners.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"missing-closing-comma"},{"text":"In the spring of 2024, our town, which had never hosted a marathon, welcomed, nearly two thousand runners.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-between-verb-object"},{"text":"In the spring of 2024, our town, which had never hosted a marathon, welcomed nearly two thousand runners.","is_correct":true},{"text":"In the spring of 2024, our town which had never hosted a marathon, welcomed nearly two thousand runners.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"missing-opening-comma"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$In the spring of 2024, our town, which had never hosted a marathon, welcomed nearly two thousand runners.$q$, $q$One comma follows the introductory phrase, and two more set off the nonessential clause "which had never hosted a marathon." Two wrong choices drop one of the commas around the clause, and one adds a comma that wrongly separates the verb "welcomed" from its object.$q$, 'draft', null)
