  ('275453e8-0274-581e-9ada-1b667365ef71', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g8-capitalization-punctuation'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Read the original sentence: "The volunteers, most of whom were high school students, planted forty trees along the creek." Which shortened quotation marks the omission correctly and still reads as a complete, grammatical sentence?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"The volunteers ... high school students, planted forty trees along the creek.\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"omission-breaks-grammar"},{"text":"\"The volunteers ... planted forty trees along the creek.\"","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"The volunteers, most of whom ... planted forty trees along the creek.\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"omission-breaks-grammar"},{"text":"\"The volunteers planted forty trees along the creek.\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"omission-not-marked"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"The volunteers ... planted forty trees along the creek."$q$, $q$Removing the whole clause "most of whom were high school students" leaves a complete sentence, and the ellipsis shows where it was. Cutting only part of the clause, as in "most of whom ...," leaves a broken phrase that no longer makes sense.$q$, 'draft', null)
